WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.800
One of the most important functions to understand&nbsp;
when using Chimera is selections. Under the Select&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.920
menu, you can select parts of the protein&nbsp;
according to Chain ID, specific chemistries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.680
residue type, and other structural features.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.720
What we have here is a hemoglobin&nbsp;
structure with the PDB ID 2hhb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:32.160
As you may know, hemoglobin contains iron ions.&nbsp;
To locate these ions, we can select by Chemistry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:43.360
Under Select, Chemistry, Element, and Other we can&nbsp;
locate the iron – Fe. Now each of the iron ions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:51.040
is encircled by a green halo. The fourth one&nbsp;
is hidden behind the ribbon. Of course, Chimera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:59.920
allows for broader selections than this. To select&nbsp;
an entire chain, we can select by Chain. Now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.720
entire chain A is highlighted in green. We can&nbsp;
play some tricks here such as selection inversion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:15.520
which inverts the selection to everything except&nbsp;
chain A. Normally, we will have the selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:24.560
mode on Replace. This means that if you select&nbsp;
chain B, chain A becomes deselected. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:34.640
we can change the selection mode to Append. Now&nbsp;
if we select Chain A, both chains are selected.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.640
Before continuing further, we must first&nbsp;
understand the small molecule data on rcsb.org.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.040
Scrolling down on the Structure&nbsp;
Summary page of our entry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.800
we eventually come to the small molecules&nbsp;
section. This section provides a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.760
ligands that interact with the protein chains but&nbsp;
are not part of them. The ligands for this protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:06.240
are heme and phosphate. The table provides&nbsp;
helpful information such as which chains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.000
molecules interact with and their abbreviations,&nbsp;
which you will see in Chimera in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:21.440
In Chimera, these ligands are accessible under&nbsp;
Select, Residue and All Non-Standard. Here, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:31.520
see the same abbreviations from rcsb.org – HEM for&nbsp;
heme and PO4 for phosphate. Chimera also includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:39.200
water molecules as HOH. With this knowledge, we&nbsp;
can now continue to explore selections in Chimera.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.720
Let's say you wanted to select the heme group&nbsp;
bound to chain B. To do this, you would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:55.120
by selecting under Residue, in the non-standard&nbsp;
category, the heme. However, this selects all four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:03.520
heme groups in the structure. But we can solve&nbsp;
this problem by intersecting the selection with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:13.200
a selection for chain B. Now only the heme group&nbsp;
associated with chain B is selected. Remember to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:20.400
reset the selection mode back to Replace and&nbsp;
clear the selection before proceeding further.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.640
Once you have made a selection in&nbsp;
Chimera, you can perform actions upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:34.320
that selection. Here we have selected chain&nbsp;
A. Under actions, we can color this chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:41.120
however we want. Notice that coloring can&nbsp;
apply selectively but defaults to All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.600
the Above. To selectively color the ribbons, we&nbsp;
can choose this option and a color of choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:55.040
Now notice that the atoms and bonds have&nbsp;
retained their original coloration. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.640
if we want to color those as well, we can&nbsp;
choose this option and the desired color.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.480
You can also use actions in Chimera to toggle&nbsp;
structural representation as well as coloration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.720
Like before I've used Intersect to select&nbsp;
the heme group associated with chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:23.280
B. Under Actions, Atoms and Bonds, we can toggle&nbsp;
the representation. For example, choosing Ball and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:30.240
Stick will change the representation as shown&nbsp;
here. If we want a more space-filling model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:37.280
we can choose Sphere. Now if you change the&nbsp;
representation to Stick, you'll notice that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.880
heme group looks much as it did in the beginning,&nbsp;
except the iron atom has sort of disappeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:52.160
However, remember that the selection mode is still&nbsp;
on Intersect, so under Chemistry, we can select&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:05:00.400
the iron atom specifically. Now under Actions we&nbsp;
can change the depiction of this atom to Sphere;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.920
and the heme group is restored to&nbsp;
its exact original representation.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.280
Let's say you wanted to examine&nbsp;
this same heme group close up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.440
but the surrounding ribbon is kind of getting in&nbsp;
your way. To solve this problem, you can start by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:27.120
selecting the entire chain B. Then under Actions,&nbsp;
you can choose Ribbon Hide. Now only the heme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:35.200
group and some surrounding residues are shown. You&nbsp;
can undo this operation by clicking Ribbon Show.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.360
Finally, it is also possible to delete parts of&nbsp;
the structure entirely that are not relevant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.040
your visualization. For example, let's say you&nbsp;
wanted to delete all parts of this structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.400
except the chain that we have previously&nbsp;
colored. To do this, you would start by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:04.400
changing the selection mode to Append and then&nbsp;
selecting chains C and D. Now under Actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:12.960
Atoms and Bonds, you can choose Delete, leaving&nbsp;
us only with the previously colored chain.

